//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
// The two-socket host the NUMA experiment actually runs on.
//
// `xeon-e5-2666v3.vx` models the same shape on an older part and cannot be
// measured: that part is only reachable as a virtualized c4.8xlarge, whose two
// NUMA nodes turn out to be cosmetic. See the note in that file. This one is a
// bare-metal instance, so the topology the guest sees is the topology the
// hardware has -- there is no hypervisor between them to synthesize it.
//
// Read from the machine on 2026-09-12, not transcribed from the SKU. `lscpu`
// reports Xeon Platinum 8275CL at 3.00GHz, 24 cores per socket, 2 sockets, 2
// threads per core; `/proc/meminfo` MemTotal = 197653284 kB; `numactl -H` two
// nodes at 96323 MB and 96696 MB with SLIT distances 10 local and 21 remote.
//
// The interconnect figure is measured rather than only derived, which is the
// thing xeon-e5-2666v3.vx cannot offer for its own. See the note on the UPI edge
// below: utils/campaign/run_numa_probe.sh measured a remote/local ratio of 2.348
// against the 2.258 this file predicts, a 4% error, and the absolute bandwidths
// corroborate the link count as well as the ratio.
//
// The SLIT distances are worth ignoring, incidentally: the virtualized c4.8xlarge
// reports the same 10 and 21 while having no locality at all. Firmware saying a
// hop is expensive is not evidence that it is.
//
//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//

// ONE NUMA domain: node0's half of the 192 GiB.
//
// `HBM` and `PEER_HBM` are the fleet's role names, not claims about the
// material -- this is DDR4 and there is no HBM near it, exactly as
// xeon-e5-2666v3.vx explains for its own DDR4. The pair says "a tile lives in
// one domain, and reaching it from the other crosses a priced link", which is
// the same relation node-8gpu.vx declares between two GPUs.
//
// spec: `numactl -H` on the box -- node 0 at 96323 MB and node 1 at 96696 MB,
// so 94 GiB each against the 96 GiB half of what the instance is sold as. 96 GiB
// is declared rather than the 94 the kernel reports, per fleet/README.md:
// declared capacity is the device, not the deployment budget, and the ~2 GiB gap
// is firmware and kernel reservation -- the same class of gap the E5 file
// records.
//
// spec: derived and then corroborated. Cascade Lake-SP has 6 DDR4 channels per
// socket at up to 2933 MT/s, so
//   6 channels x 2933 MT/s x 8 B = 140.8 GB/s per socket.
// That is one socket's memory controllers' peak and not an achievable rate: the
// probe measured 121 GB/s reading node-local memory with 48 threads, which is
// 86% of it. Declared at the peak, because that is what the hardware can do and
// the model is meant to over-predict a real transfer rather than track it.
//
// Staying BELOW the peak is the part that matters. A bind that measures above
// one socket's ceiling is a bind that is not confining anything, which is how
// the c4.8xlarge gave itself away.
Memory HBM {
  capacity: 96 GiB, bandwidth: 140 GB/s, managed: explicit, scope: device, node: 0
}

// node1's half. A space of its own, because that is the whole content of NUMA:
// the bytes are reachable, and reaching them costs something that reaching local
// bytes does not.
//
// spec: as `HBM` above -- the two sockets are the same part.
//
// `node: 1` is what a backend acts on. Everything else here lets the compiler admit or refuse
// a placement; this says where to put the bytes, and without it an admitted placement lands
// wherever the first touch happened to be.
Memory PEER_HBM {
  capacity: 96 GiB, bandwidth: 140 GB/s, managed: explicit, scope: device, node: 1
}

// The last-level cache. `L2` names the role (last level on die) rather than the
// level number, which on this part is L3.
//
// spec: `lscpu -C` on the box -- L3, ONE-SIZE 35.8M, ALL-SIZE 71.5M, 11-way, 64 B
// line, two instances. So 35.8 MiB per socket, and the larger of the two figures
// the SKU is quoted at: 35.75 MiB is 26 cores' worth of Cascade Lake's 1.375 MiB
// per core against 24 enabled cores, so two cores are present and disabled.
//
// ONE socket's LLC is declared rather than both, because a tile is resident in
// one socket's cache and the other socket's copy is a coherence problem rather
// than extra capacity.
//
// Written in KiB because 35.75 MiB is not a whole number of MiB and the parser
// refuses a fractional one -- 35.75 x 1024 = 36608 KiB. `lscpu -C` prints 35.8M,
// which is its own rounding of the same number.
//
// spec: UNVERIFIED bandwidth. Intel publishes no L3 figure for this part; 400
// GB/s is a placeholder of the right order for a mesh-interconnect LLC on a
// 24-core die and no claim rests on it.
Memory L2 {
  within: Memory::HBM, capacity: 36608 KiB, bandwidth: 400 GB/s, managed: cached, scope: device
}

// No `SMEM`, for the reason xeon-e5-2666v3.vx sets out at length: the fleet's
// third space is a software-managed scratchpad, x86 has none, and declaring the
// per-core cache under that name would admit a program that wanted guaranteed
// residency and tell it something false. A program that wants a scratchpad
// cannot have one here and finds out at compile time.

// The host seam is degenerate here in the same way: the device IS the host, so
// `Memory::CPU_DRAM` and `Memory::HBM` name the same DDR4 and a transfer is a
// memcpy. Declared at one node's rate, and the model should over-predict it by
// at least 2x because a memcpy reads and writes and so moves two bytes of
// traffic per byte copied.
//
// THE UPI EDGE IS WHAT THIS FILE EXISTS FOR, and it is now measured rather than
// assumed. Cascade Lake carries UPI at 10.4 GT/s, 2 bytes per transfer, so
//   10.4 GT/s x 2 B = 20.8 GB/s per link per direction,
// and the link count was the open question: three links give 62.4 GB/s, two give
// 41.6, and this file had to guess three.
//
// The measurement settles it two ways. `lspci` shows three UPI register devices
// per socket (buses 7a and f3). And the probe measured 51.55 GB/s reading across
// the link, in both directions, which two links could not carry at all -- 41.6
// is their ceiling. Three links it is, at 83% of their combined peak.
//
// Against a node's own 140 GB/s this predicts a remote access costs 2.258x a
// local one. The probe measured 2.348x, a 4% error. That is the figure this
// whole file existed to produce, and it is the first interconnect cost in
// fleet/ that has been checked against the hardware rather than derived and
// left alone.
//
// Re-run with: utils/campaign/run_numa_probe.sh (MACHINE= defaults to this file).
Topology Device {
  // spec: `lscpu` on the box -- Intel Cascade Lake-SP, as a compute target rather than a host.
  arch: x86_64,
  memory: Memory::HBM,
  visible: [Memory::HBM, Memory::PEER_HBM, Memory::L2],
  transfer Memory::CPU_DRAM -> Memory::HBM : 140 GB/s,
  // The same memcpy in the other direction, declared at the same peak and UNVERIFIED for
  // the reason above. Reading a placed tensor back is how a program says it still holds it.
  transfer Memory::HBM -> Memory::CPU_DRAM : 140 GB/s,
  transfer Memory::HBM -> Memory::L2,
  transfer Memory::HBM -> Memory::PEER_HBM : 62 GB/s,
  transfer Memory::PEER_HBM -> Memory::HBM : 62 GB/s
}
